# Docker reads .dockerignore from the BUILD CONTEXT root, which is the monorepo root (the
# root `Dockerfile` explains why). The previous apps/web/.dockerignore is therefore dead and
# has been removed — leaving it would look like it were in force while Docker ignored it.

# Dependencies — reinstalled from the lockfile inside the image.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output
.next
**/.next
out
**/out
*.tsbuildinfo

# Runtime state: the SQLite DB and cloned workspaces live on a mounted disk,
# never in the image.
apps/web/data
**/data/workspaces

.git
.gitignore
.github

# Not part of the web image.
desktop
docs
benchmarks

# `Dockerfile` IS NOT EXCLUDED, deliberately, and this is not an oversight to tidy up.
#
# With `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1` the build is handled by an external BuildKit frontend,
# and a frontend resolves the dockerfile BY NAME — which, depending on how the platform
# invokes the build, can mean reading it back out of the context. A dockerignored dockerfile
# then produces exactly the error Render deployed with:
#
#   failed to solve: failed to read dockerfile: open Dockerfile: no such file or directory
#
# It is one 7.5 kB text file. Excluding it saves nothing and can cost the whole deploy.
# `**/docker-compose.yml` stays excluded because nothing ever reads it during a build.
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
**/docker-compose.yml

npm-debug.log*
*.log
.env
.env*.local
*.md
.DS_Store
coverage
.vscode
.idea

# Test suites: the image runs the app, never the tests. Next's standalone writer copies the
# app directory wholesale, so without this the container ships apps/web/tests too — same
# finding as apps/desktop/scripts/build/asset-copy.ts EXCLUDED_PATHS, other packaging path.
# The Dockerfile builds and never invokes vitest, so nothing in the build needs these.
apps/*/tests
packages/*/tests
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx
